WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
File processing should be defensive because parsers are part of the attack surface.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:15.620
CSV, JSON, XML, PDF, images, archives, and office documents each have different structure, metadata, resource behavior, and parser dependencies.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.520
A file that passes upload validation may still fail during parsing.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:27.100
The processor should expect malformed input, unsupported versions, missing fields, excessive nesting, and unexpected content.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.680
Archive and decompression workflows need particular care.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:41.620
Archives may contain many files, deeply nested directories, large expanded content, unsafe paths, duplicate names, symlinks, or unexpected structures.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:51.920
Processing should validate entries before extraction, enforce total expanded size limits, reject unsafe paths, and use temporary working directories that are cleaned up reliably.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.260
Do not let archive contents decide where files are written.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:05.240
Resource exhaustion can occur through large files, complex documents, image dimensions, nested structures, decompression ratios, or expensive parser behavior.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:13.220
The application should set timeouts, memory limits, size limits, item count limits, and concurrency controls that match the workflow.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:20.880
Sandboxing at a high level may be appropriate for risky parsers or untrusted document conversions, especially when third-party tools are involved.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.580
Errors, logs, and generated artifacts should avoid leaking sensitive file data.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.240
A parser exception should not dump private document contents.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.900
A failed conversion should not leave temporary files behind.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.360
A generated preview should not include hidden fields the user was not allowed to see.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:45.580
File processing is not finished when parsing succeeds; it is finished when accepted output is stored safely and temporary material is cleaned up.
